Submissions: fable5 (62KB), opus5 (47KB), grok46 (46KB), sol56 (44KB), gpt55 (39KB); coordinator blind submission committed pre-read (its Q1 first-lane — the coprime-leaders test — is REFUTED by 17(ppppp); noted).

## 1. Consensus (5/5)

**Q1 (K16) — SETTLED by 17(ppppp), and re-typed.** All five affirm: the packet's three routes (weighted Fröberg, triangular coprime-leaders, degree-only Bézout) are PROVED IMPOSSIBLE, not unfinished; the length L_t = 2·binom(3t+1,t−1) is a theorem (CI-SERIES); the residual is CRITERION RANK (a non-vanishing along the rank-1 curve of the (t−1)×2 matrix (C_r,B_r)); Z-EMPTY_t is NOT a better all-t target. NEW (Fable, Sol, GPT-5.5): (V0) is STRONGER than JC2 needs — the JC2-relevant all-t target is (8.1) = τ_t ∈ radical(I_{t,+}) ALONE (z-type pairs put the Jacobian line through the SIMPLE place, so Z-EMPTY is JC2-irrelevant). RECOMMENDATION: keep the launched k16-rank-criterion lane (it proves (V0), sufficient for (8.1)); if RANK stalls, fall back to τ_t ∈ radical directly (Rabinowitsch on the cone). DEMOTE (V0) from flagship to fixed-t instrument.

**Q2/Q4 — case (A) is NOT isolated; it is the head of a ray (the single most valuable object, 4/5).** Fable: case (A) = (27,18; 21; 8; k=4) is the K=9 member of the two-point ray (3K, 2K; 3K−6; K−1; k=4), (δ₂',δ₁')=(−1,0); its K=7,8 members are the OPEN rows (147,98)→(21,14; 15; 6; 4) and (168,112)→(24,16; 18; 7; 4) — and (24,16) is ALSO the D=108 no-split datum (17(fffff)). So case (A) of (99,66), the D=108 no-split arm, and the three δ₁'=0 two-point rows are ONE OBJECT — the k=4 ray. Decide it STRUCTURALLY, uniformly in K, not by the 110–256 MB full-basis Gröbner runs (which is why 17(nnnnn) timed out). Opus states it as the UNSPLIT-CONFIGURATION LEMMA: for a u_s≥2 unsplit skeleton, the low coefficient B₂ is either constant (then h|J, the pair is composite) or lies in an explicitly bounded space on which the Jacobian system is inconsistent — its hard half is a Jacobian identity, not a Gröbner basis. This closes the 17(tttt) QUALIFICATION (case A), D=108, and the δ₁'=0 rows together.

**Q3 ((H1)) — disc dead; the census is the deliverable; ONE new theorem lead (Opus).** disc is refuted (a nondegeneracy certificate makes the block INVERTIBLE hence solvable — it cannot be the reason for inconsistency). The right TYPE is a quantity nonzero exactly when an inhomogeneous row SURVIVES elimination. Opus's candidate: the ladder pivot closed form 9(99−3n−11i) = (n/d_s)(n − u_s·j − d_s·i), 1≤j≤v_s, gives a skeleton predicate NONRES(S) :≡ d_s ∤ u_s·j for all 1≤j≤v_s ⟺ u_s > (g−1)v_s (g = gcd(u_s,v_s)) — a COPRIMALITY reason for inconsistency, arithmetic, cheap to test across the census. The census deliverable (Sol/Opus): 166 groups / ~274 reduced pairs at D≤200; a uniform theorem still needs the augmented Schur/Fitting row, which the obstruction-core proved is NOT determined by skeleton data alone (17(lllll)).

**N1/N2 (paper-grade (99,66)).** N2 IS closable: a source read of Xu §8(i) (its one-sentence elimination of δ∈{4/3,3/2,5/3,7/3}, Cor 7.5-shaped, ~4 finite face-ODE runs) + the Galois den(δ)≤u_s already PROMOTED (17(rrr)). N1 is NOT source-closable (Fable, Sol, Grok): Moh's printed conditions (1)–(13) leave EIGHT admissible V-assignments at (99,66); his private-program assertion selects one. So a DEGREE-WIDE (99,66) theorem is genuinely OPEN — a real hole in Moh's published work; the SKELETON theorem (fixed V=(8,8)) is what THEOREM 8.1 delivers.

## 2. Q4 — the program LEAKS; honest scope (Grok, Sol, GPT-5.5)

