## 2. Charged Setting

## 3. Target A: Gamma And The Nonzerodivisor

### 3.1 Exact Reformulation

Let `N` be the `(t-1) x 2` matrix over `P_t` with rows `(C_r,B_r)`.  The charged
FITT theorem identifies the 0-th Fitting ideal of `M_t/(G)M_t`, with
`M_t=S_t/(T_{t,2t-1})`, as

```text
F_t = I_2(N) + (W_1,...,W_{t-1}) + (X_rs)_{r<s},
W_r = a_0 C_r^2 - b_0 B_r C_r + c_0 B_r^2.
```

The explicit eliminant certificate, reproduced here in
`box/k16nzd-20260903/eliminant_certificate.out`, is the identity

```text
W_r = B_r^2 T_{t,2t-1}
      - G_r (a_0 G_r - 2 a_0 C_r + b_0 B_r).
```

The frozen driver prints

```text
W - [B^2 Q_0 - G(a0 G - 2a0 C + b0 B)] = 0
Res_b3(Q_0,G) = B^2 c0 - B C b0 + C^2 a0 = W.
```

At a rank-1 point `p` of `N`, with kernel spanned by `(1:beta)`, we have
`C_r(p)=-beta B_r(p)`.  Therefore

```text
W_r(p) = B_r(p)^2 Q_t(p,beta),
X_rs(p) = B_r(p)B_s(p) Q_t(p,beta),
Q_t(p,beta) = a_0 beta^2 + b_0 beta + c_0
             = T_{t,2t-1}(p,beta).
```

Consequently, after clause (i) excludes nonzero rank-0 points, Target A is
exactly:

```text
Q_t(p,beta) != 0
for every nonzero p in Gamma = V(I_2(N)) with rank N(p)=1.
```

Equivalently, `T_{t,2t-1}` is a nonzerodivisor on
`S_t/(G_1,...,G_{t-1})`.

### 3.2 EN-Curve Parametrisation

The usable parametrisation is the incidence description, not a closed formula
for all coordinates.  Define

```text
Sigma_t = V(C_r + beta B_r, r=1..t-1)
          inside Spec P_t x A^1_beta.
```

On the open where not all `B_r` vanish, projection
`Sigma_t -> Gamma=V(I_2(N))` is an isomorphism over the rank-1 locus and beta is
the kernel coordinate.  Clause (i), or the stronger charged B-HSOP statement,
removes nonzero points with all `B_r=C_r=0`; it also removes the kernel
`(0:1)` obstruction.  Thus the card's "kernel is `(1:beta)` and `(C_r,B_r)`
are proportional" is exactly this incidence scheme.

What is missing is a closed lower-dimensional base with closed-form coordinates
for the rank-1 points.  The charged rank report explains why: the `B_r,C_r`
