+
+## 2. Q1 - K16
+
+**Answer.** The next K16 idea should be a norm/resultant proof over the
+projectivized Eagon-Northcott incidence scheme. Do not spend the next serious
+lane on another monolithic tail GB at `t=8`; use fixed `t` only to manufacture
+the norm data and controls.
+
+Typed state:
+
+```text
+Known:
+  For t>=3, after clause (i), the target is exactly
+    Q_t(p,beta)=a_0 beta^2+b_0 beta+c_0 != 0
+  at every nonzero rank-one point of
+    Sigma_t = V(u C_r + v B_r, 1<=r<t)
+  over every factor of A_t.  Equivalently,
+    sqrt(I_2(N)+(W_1,...,W_{t-1})) = m
+  factorwise once rank-zero points are excluded.
+
+Bounded quantity:
+  the finite weighted projective scheme has degree
+    d_Gamma(t) = (binom(3t+1,t-1)-binom(2t,t-1))/(t+1).
+  The nonvanishing is a product of d_Gamma(t) scalar tests, not a new
+  positive-dimensional search.
+
+Wrong routes:
+  LENGTH-SPLIT is conditional on finiteness and cannot prove it.
+  No monomial order can give the required coprime leaders for all t.
+  W_1 alone is not a chart-safe invariant.
+```
+
+The structural proof object should be
+
+```text
+N_t = Norm_{R_t/A_t}(\widehat Q_t),
+widehat Q_t = a_0 v^2 + b_0 u v + c_0 u^2,
+R_t = coordinate algebra of Proj Sigma_t after removing the rank-zero locus.
+```
+
+The theorem to prove is `N_t != 0` for every integer `t>=3`, factorwise in
+`A_t`. This is better typed than "prove no zeros on Gamma" because it replaces
+exponentially many algebraic point values by one determinant in an explicit
+finite algebra. It also gives a falsifier: `N_t=0` produces an actual
+rank-one point with top-row vanishing.
+
+The reason I like this target is that all current K16 inputs point to it. The
+EN complex already supplies the finite algebra and degree. FITT supplies the
+eliminant identity. The coefficient recursion supplies an exact finite
+presentation of `a_r,b_r,c_r,B_r,C_r`. What is missing is not another row but
+the determinant of multiplication by `widehat Q_t` in the finite projective
+rank-one algebra.
+
+Cheapest test:
+
+```text
+Build R_t on the homogeneous incidence presentation
+  u C_r + v B_r = 0
+with one safe affine chart at a time, using the irrelevant saturation only as
+a declared component operation.  For t=3,4 exact, compute the determinant of
+multiplication by widehat Q_t in each chart algebra and compare with the
+banked component decomposition.  Wall: <10 min, <4 GB.  Then run t=5 modulo
+one good prime with the same determinant output, not primdecGTZ.  Wall target:
+<30 min.
+```
+
+Interpretation:
+
+```text
+If t=3,4 determinants reproduce the known nonzero component scalars and t=5
+gives a compact factored determinant, launch the all-t determinant-recursion
+lane.
+
+If the determinant exists but is huge and structureless at t=5, switch to a
+flat degeneration proof of the same norm.
+
+If any determinant is zero in a factor where existing reports say the top row
+is nonzero, the ring/component map is wrong; stop and audit the incidence
+construction.
+
+If t>=7 gives zero, extract the point and test the original tail rows.  That
+would be a real K16 obstruction, not a counterexample to JC2 until routed back.
+```
+
+Two implementation cautions. First, `Proj Gamma` here is a finite weighted
+scheme; do not import the false intuition that a positive-degree section on a
+projective curve must vanish. The affine cone is one-dimensional, so the
+projective base is zero-dimensional. Second, the norm must be factorwise over
+`A_t`; split indices cannot be handled by inverting a denominator that vanishes
+on one factor.
+
+I would keep direct `(8.1)` as the fallback, but it currently has less
+structure. A Rabinowitsch certificate for
+`tau_t in sqrt(I_{t,+})` would be ideal; the banked evidence says no closed
+power/cofactor family is visible through `t<=7`, and interpolation is unsafe
+because the spine depth grows with `t`. The norm proof attacks the stronger
+target, but it attacks the one object the campaign has actually exposed.
+
+## 3. Q2 - Degree-wide (99,66) / N1
+
+**Answer.** Yes, killing the five remaining N1 skeletons is the right route for
+the degree-wide `(99,66)` theorem. A single uniform obstruction across all
+eight V-assignments is not the cheapest next proof. There may be shared
+compiler templates, especially for S2/S3/S7, but the obstruction itself is
+not uniform in the mathematical sense yet.
+
+Typed state:
+
+```text
+Exact census:
+  8 Moh (1)--(13)-admissible skeletons at (99,66).
+
+Dead:
+  S8 = V=(8,8), all three configurations dead.
+  S6 = corrected exponent ell=8, exact-Q unit ideal.
+  S5 = exact-Q unit ideal.
+
+Open:
+  S1,S2,S3,S4,S7.
+```
+
+The five open rows are not one chart in disguise. They have different strict
+split windows, different surviving face partitions, and different corrected
+Prop. 6.3 exponents:
+
+```text
+S1: u_s=2, v_s=9, ell=6, split survivor delta=4 [1,1],
+    no-split systems 110..114 parameters, 7 partitions.
+
+S2/S3/S7: u_s=4, v_s=7, ell=2, shared split-order patterns
+    delta=3/2 and 5/3, but different V2' / K' / boxes.
+
+S4: u_s=3, v_s=8, ell=4, split survivors delta=2 [2,1] and 5/2 [1^3],
+    but order data differ from the killed K=9 case(A) chart.
+```
+
+That heterogeneity is enough to reject "one scalar obstruction for all eight"
+as the first move. The correct commonality is operational: exact
+face-parametrization, top/corner pins, lower-band parametrization, staged row
+files, modular screens, and exact-Q replay. The proof theorem may emerge from
+the five certificates, but it should not be assumed before the certificates
+exist.
+
+Moh citation status:
+
+```text
+Allowed:
+  Cite Moh/Xu for the source inequalities and local constructions only after
+  stating the exact hypotheses.  Cite the frozen enumerator plus source-gated
+  conditions for the statement "these are the eight (1)--(13)-admissible
+  assignments at (99,66)" once independently reviewed.
+
+Not allowed:
+  Cite Moh for "N1 selects V=(8,8)" or for the degree-wide theorem.  The
+  printed/public material does not supply that selection.  Treat it as a real
+  published gap, not a missing footnote.
+```
+
+Cheapest test:
+
+```text
+Continue the running batch3 lane while its receipt remains live.  If it fails
+or returns partial, relaunch a narrowed S1-first lane:
+  (a) build the strict split joint chart for delta=4 [1,1];
+  (b) finish all seven no-split partitions using ell=6;
+  (c) exact-Q replay only after three-prime screens.
+Expected desk formation check: S1 one-block rows already exist except the
+all-simple builder; the first split-joint chart is the cheapest missing object.
+```
+
+If S1 dies, I would next group S2/S3/S7 because their split face shapes are the
+same up to exponent replacements. S4 is neither first nor last by theory; it
+is a good second client only if batch3 has already solved the S1 formation
+wall.
+
+## 4. Q3 - (H1) General Census
+
+**Answer.** Run the batched guided-GB sweep, but do it as a certificate factory
+with theorem extraction, not as blind compute. A uniform pin+parametrization
+theorem is valuable only after the sweep identifies which pins recur. The
+augmented-Schur-row theorem is the right eventual mathematics, but it is not
+priced until row data says which augmented row survives.
+
+Typed state:
+
+```text
+Cohort:
+  296 sharpened rows / 166 groups for D<=200, u_s>=2.
+
+Dead shortcuts:
+  discriminant nonvanishing alone gives invertibility/solvability, not
+  inconsistency;
+  NONRES is true on 192/274 screened pairs but kills 0/274 by itself.
+
+Validated kill pattern:
+  case-specific pins + exact lower-band parametrization can turn a timeout
+  chart into a 36-variable exact-Q unit ideal.
+```
+
+Price:
+
+```text
+Phase 0, manifest/queue:
+  0.5 day engineering.  Produce one row-chart manifest per (skeleton,delta,
+  partition, split/no-split) with ring, weights, localizers, source conditions,
+  expected rows, and artifact hashes.
+
+Phase 1, pilot:
+  12 charts: D=108 incidence control, (99,66) delta=2, (99,66) delta=5/2,
+  the D=144 resonance false-NONRES row, and eight varied u_s>=2 rows.
+  Target: 4-8 hours on a modest AWS box or parallel local-equivalent cores;
+  local desk only for individual formation smoke tests under 10 min.
+
+Phase 2, sweep:
+  296 rows are too many for one monolith.  With staged formation and three-prime
+  screens, expect 1-3 AWS days at 64-128 vCPU if most charts reduce like K=8/K=9;
+  expect a theorem redesign if more than 20% remain over 60 variables after
+  parametrization or if exact-Q replay exceeds 30 min on more than 10% of
+  prime-unit charts.
+
+Phase 3, extraction:
+  Mine all unit certificates for the affine Schur row: which row family, which
+  localizer power, which pin, and which low-band variables disappear.  This is
+  the input to a theorem, not the precondition for the sweep.
+```
+
+Cheapest discriminator:
+
+```text
+Run the 12-chart pilot.  If >=8/12 die with exact-Q unit certificates after
+lower-band parametrization, sweep.  If the resonance row survives all obvious
+augmented rows or stays positive-dimensional, stop the broad sweep and attack
+the augmented-Schur theorem directly on that row.  If formation, not solving,
+dominates again, invest in the systems upgrade below before adding lanes.
+```
+
+The key discipline is to keep a positive-dimensional necessary chart from
+being overread. A unit ideal in a necessary over-approximation kills a row; a
+nonunit only says the over-approximation was not enough.
